# Continuous Probability Distributions: Industry Applications

## Project Description

**Continuous Probability Distributions: Industry Applications** is an interactive, web-based educational application designed to bridge the gap between theoretical statistics and real-world Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning (AIML) applications.

The application helps undergraduate students understand continuous probability distributions through practical industry scenarios, interactive calculations, mathematical formulas, and dynamic visualizations.

It consists of three major learning modules:

### 1. Normal Distribution – Manufacturing Quality Control
Demonstrates how the Normal Distribution is used to analyze manufactured component dimensions and maintain quality standards. Students can calculate probability density, specification-limit probabilities, defective percentages, and z-scores using interactive inputs and a dynamic bell curve.

### 2. Exponential Distribution – Cloud Request Inter-arrival Time
Explores how the Exponential Distribution models the time between consecutive cloud service requests under a constant-rate Poisson arrival process. Students can calculate mean inter-arrival time, variance, cumulative probability, survival probability, and conditional probability while understanding the memoryless property.

### 3. Central Limit Theorem – Warehouse Processing Time
Demonstrates how sample averages of warehouse order-processing times approach an approximately Normal distribution as sample size increases. Students can explore population mean, standard deviation, sample size, standard error, z-scores, and probabilities through interactive calculations and visualizations.

## Key Features
- Interactive input parameters and automatic calculations.
- Mathematical formulas with simple explanations.
- Dynamic probability distribution graphs.
- Real-world industry-based problem statements.
- Probability and statistical analysis.
- Z-score calculations and interpretations.
- Input validation and unit conversions.
- Beginner-friendly explanations and visual learning.

## Technology Used
- HTML
- CSS
- JavaScript
- SVG-based dynamic visualizations

## Objective

The primary objective is to make statistical concepts easier to understand by connecting mathematical theory with practical applications in manufacturing, cloud computing, and warehouse operations. It provides students with a hands-on learning environment to explore how probability distributions support data analysis and decision-making in AIML.
